## What the run was

A **single-day, closing-session backtest** (2026-01-05) where the PM **passed on all 6 analyst picks** — the only execution was the mechanical SPY benchmark sweep ($8,940.36 into SPY, $1,059.64 cash buffer). Realized NAV finished flat at $10,000 (0.00%, 0.00 alpha vs SPY).

Because it's a one-day all-pass run, the standard realized metrics are degenerate (win rate 0/0, Sharpe/profit-factor undefined), so I stated that plainly rather than fabricate numbers.

## Where the value actually is

All checkpoint dates are now ~6 months past, so I fetched point-in-time forward prices for all 6 rejected picks and built the counterfactual the run itself left empty (`outcomes.json` had every checkpoint as `null`). Highlights:

- **The bear debate was the MVP.** It killed UNH (the highest raw-score pick at 7.85) after proving two thesis pillars false. The bear forecast *"−20–25% on a Jan-27 earnings guide-down"* — UNH then closed **−19.6% on 2026-01-27**. Avoided **−16.60%**.
- **Passing the whole book was correct.** An equal-weight basket of the 6 picks would have returned **−5.92%** at horizon; 0 of 6 hit target.
- **Two disasters dodged:** UNH −16.6% and VRNS (sentiment) −21.8% (broke the −20% stop).
- **One honest counterpoint:** RKLB (quant) was the best would-have performer at +11.33% but was hard-rejected pre-debate by the "don't chase >15% above SMA200" guardrail — a known false-positive cost worth tracking, though RKLB also round-tripped and required precise exit timing.

The report follows the skill's template (Performance, Agent Leaderboard, Debate Impact, Monthly Breakdown, Key Observations, Recommendations) plus a clearly-labeled Counterfactual section, and flags two follow-ups: re-examine the quant chase guardrail if winning chase-rejections accumulate, and run a multi-week window for meaningful Sharpe/drawdown numbers.
